#Dictionaries: Storing data in key-value pairs
#A dictionary is a collection of key-value pairs. Each key is unique and is used to access the corresponding value.
#Dictionaries are defined using curly braces {} and key-value pairs are separated by a colon :.

#Make a dictionary that is called player_stats and has the following key-value pairs:
player_stats = {
    #Dictionary values must be health, mana, and stamina. The values for each key should be a number between 0 and 100.
    "health": 100, 
    "mana": 50,
    "stamina": 75
}

#Lists VS Dictionaries
#Lists are ordered collections of items, while dictionaries are unordered collections of key-value pairs. Lists are accessed using index numbers, while dictionaries are accessed using keys. Lists can contain duplicate values, while dictionary keys must be unique.
#Dictionaries are useful when you want to associate specific values with unique keys, while lists are useful when you want to store a collection of items in a specific order.


#Accessing dictionary values
#print(player_stats["health"]) #This will print 100


#Add and update dictionary values ?
#You can add a new key-value pair to a dictionary by using the assignment operator =. You can also update the value of an existing key by assigning a new value to it.
#You can add a new key-value pair to the player_stats dictionary like this:
player_stats["gold"] = 1000
player_stats["health"] = 90 #This will update the value of the "health" key to 90
player_stats["mana"] = 75 #This will update the value of the "mana" key to 75
print(player_stats) #This will print the updated dictionary with the new key-value pair and updated values for existing keys

#Keys() and Values() methods
#The keys() method returns a view object that displays a list of all the keys in the dictionary. The values() method returns a view object that displays a list of all the values in the dictionary.
print(player_stats.keys()) #This will print a list of all the keys in the player_stats dictionary
print(player_stats.values()) #This will print a list of all the values in the player_stats dictionary
# The values() method can also be used to get the value of a specific key in the dictionary. For example, to get the value of the "health" key, you can use player_stats["health"].

#How we can loop through a dictionary
#You can loop through a dictionary using a for loop. You can loop through the keys, the values, or both the keys and values of the dictionary. Here are some examples:
#Looping through the keys of the dictionary
for key in player_stats:
    print(key) #This will print each key in the player_stats dictionary

#Looping through the values of the dictionary
for value in player_stats.values():
    print(value) #This will print each value in the player_stats dictionary

#Looping through both keys and values of the dictionary
for key, value in player_stats.items():
    print(key, value) #This will print each key-value pair in the player_stats dictionary

    #Nested Dictionaries
#A nested dictionary is a dictionary that contains another dictionary as a value. You can access the values of a nested dictionary by using multiple keys. Here is an example of a nested dictionary:
player_stats = {
    
    "player1": {
        "health": 100,
        "mana": 50,
        "stamina": 75
    },
    "player2": {
        "health": 90,
        "mana": 75,
        "stamina": 80
    }
}
#Accessing values in a nested dictionary
print(player_stats["player1"]["health"]) #This will print 100
print(player_stats["player2"]["mana"]) #This will print 75
print(player_stats["player1"]["stamina"]) #This will print 75
print(player_stats["player2"]["stamina"]) #This will print 80`

